    "knowledge_point_id": "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3",
    "knowledge_point": "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14059427/assets/ca290e8fd51edddf4381ec267b8e0a5692d0e52bd2a36b7d43ab89cbeb5cf00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?",
    "worked_solution": "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
